            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=\\pm2,\\ \\pm3$. Use $u=x^2$.",
              "worked": "Let $u=x^2$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}u^2-13u+36&=0\\\\(u-4)(u-9)&=0\\\\u&=4\\text{ or }9\\end{aligned}$$\nSubstitute back:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x^2&=4\\text{ or }9\\\\x&=\\pm2\\text{ or }\\pm3\\end{aligned}$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 48
          },
          {
            "id": "p11-q9-b",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "b",
            "prompt": "$x^4+13x^2+36=0$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "No real solution: substituting $u=x^2$ gives $u=-4$ or $-9$.",
              "worked": "Let $u=x^2$.\n$$\\begin{aligned}u^2+13u+36&=0\\\\(u+4)(u+9)&=0\\\\u&=-4\\text{ or }-9\\\\x^2&=-4\\text{ or }-9\\end{aligned}$$\nNeither is possible for real $x$, since a real square is nonnegative. There is no real solution.",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 43
          }
        ]
